# Keep the build context small and free of secrets/runtime state.
#
# Matching semantics: Docker accepts `**/` as "any number of directories,
# including zero", while a bare filepath.Match reading treats `*` as
# not-crossing-`/`. Each entry is therefore written twice (bare + `**/`) so
# the exclusion holds under either reading for every path we care about;
# parent-directory matches also exclude whole subtrees.
.git
.gitignore
.github
.venv
venv
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.pyc
**/*.pyc
*.db
**/*.db
*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
**/*.sqlite3
*.log
**/*.log
.env
.env.*
.envrc
**/.envrc

# Secrets. From T7 this repo holds an L1 private key and a Lighter API private
# key; an accidental `COPY . .` must not be able to ship them into an image.
# The secret patterns get an extra depth level on purpose: Docker's matcher
# already treats `**/` as any-depth, but a plain filepath.Match reading stops
# at one level, and a leaked trading key is unrecoverable while the redundant
# line costs nothing.
*.key
**/*.key
**/**/*.key
*.pem
**/*.pem
**/**/*.pem
*.p12
**/*.p12
**/**/*.p12
id_rsa*
**/id_rsa*
**/**/id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
**/id_ed25519*
**/**/id_ed25519*
secrets/
**/secrets/

# Runtime state written by the bot, never build input. Kept root-relative on
# purpose: a recursive `**/data/` would also drop an `app/data/` fixture dir.
data/
logs/

# Tests run in CI; docs are read on GitHub. Neither belongs in the image.
tests
docs

# These describe the build rather than its content, and `COPY . .` would
# otherwise bake them into the image.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
